Roughly (Angular 18 version):

```ts
export class HashLocationStrategy extends LocationStrategy implements OnDestroy {
  private _baseHref: string|null = null;
  private _removeListenerFns: (() => void)[] = [];

  constructor(private _platformLocation: PlatformLocation, @Inject(APP_BASE_HREF) _baseHref?: string) {
    super();
    if (_baseHref != null) {
      this._baseHref = _baseHref;
    }
    ...
  }

  override path(external = false): string {
    // the hash value is the location hash minus the leading '#'
    let hash = this._platformLocation.hash ?? '#';
    // remove the '#' char at the start and return
    const hashIndex = hash.indexOf('#');
    hash = hashIndex >= 0 ? hash.substring(hashIndex + 1) : '';
    // in HTML5 mode, the base href is prepended to the path
    return this._toExternalPath(hash, external);  // not exact
  }
  ...
}
```

Hmm I don't remember exactly. Let me just read the actual installed source in node_modules. That's the reliable way.

Actually, alternative much simpler approach: DON'T use Angular Router navigation via location at all. Implement my own tiny routing:

- AppComponent subscribes to `window.onhashchange` (or router events) and sets the current screen.
- Menubar links are plain `<a href="#manufacturer">` anchors. Clicking them changes the hash (browser default), triggering hashchange → app switches screen.
- Initial load: read location.hash, default to manufacturer.

This satisfies "hash-based routing" (the acceptance criteria says "the menubar (id gwt-debug-menubar) with links whose href contains the GWT history tokens (manufacturer, rating, report, see NameTokens.java); use hash-based routing"). It doesn't strictly require the Angular Router. But using Angular Router would be cleaner. However, the task says fixed src/main.ts bootstraps appConfig from src/app/app.config.ts — we control app.config.ts. We could still register Router with a custom strategy.

Hmm, but a hand-rolled router is risky re: "hash-based routing" acceptance. A custom LocationStrategy extending HashLocationStrategy is a recognized Angular pattern. Let me read the Angular 18.2 HashLocationStrategy source from node_modules to be precise.

Also need to check `provideRouter(routes)` with `withHashLocation()` — Angular 18 has `withHashLocation()` feature. I can provide a custom strategy via `{provide: LocationStrategy, useClass: MyStrategy}`.

