Jackson coerces strings to Long/Integer fine. And the rating POST works with a minimal car `{id}` — the server fills in the rest.

So my plan: parse API responses with a reviver-style regex so all `"id": <big>` become strings, keep ids as `string` in the models, and send them back as strings (or embed in URL paths).

Now let me write the app. I'll create the files.

First `src/app/core/models.ts`: I'll name interfaces after the DTOs.

```ts
export interface ManufacturerDto {
  id: string | null;
  name: string;
  cars?: CarDto[] | null;
}
...
```

`src/app/core/api.service.ts`:

```ts
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { CarDto, ManufacturerDto, ManufacturerRatingDto, RatingDto } from './models';

const API_ROOT = '/api';

function parseJson<T>(text: string): T {
  // Preserve 64-bit datastore ids exactly by keeping them as strings.
  const safe = text.replace(/"id"\s*:\s*(\d+)/g, '"id":"$1"');
  return JSON.parse(safe) as T;
}

@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class ApiService {
  ...
}
```

Wait — careful: `"carProperties":{"id":4785074604081152,...}` — the regex `"id"\s*:\s*(\d+)` matches all. But also might match inside a string value that literally contains `"id":123`. Fine.

Hmm, one more: the regex would also transform `"id":123` → `"id":"123"` in REQUEST bodies? No — requests are built from TS objects; JSON.stringify of string ids produces `"id":"506..."` naturally. ✓

Methods:

```ts
async getManufacturers(): Promise<ManufacturerDto[]>
async saveManufacturer(dto: ManufacturerDto): Promise<ManufacturerDto>  // POST /api/manufacturers
async deleteManufacturer(id: string): Promise<void>                     // DELETE /api/manufacturers/{id}
async getAverageRatings(): Promise<ManufacturerRatingDto[]>             // GET /api/manufacturers/ratings
async getRatings(): Promise<RatingDto[]>
async saveRating(dto: RatingDto): Promise<RatingDto>                    // POST /api/ratings
async deleteRating(id: string): Promise<void>
async getCars(): Promise<CarDto[]>                                      // GET /api/cars?offset=0&limit=-1
```

Error handling: fetch doesn't throw on 4xx/5xx — check response.ok, throw Error with status. Callers catch → error message.

Wait — GWT ErrorHandlerAsyncCallback: on failure shows an error message. What text? Let me not worry — I'll use a generic message. Let me check if there's an app properties... The GWT client util ErrorHandlerAsyncCallback isn't in the provided sources. I'll write "Oops, something went wrong while contacting the server." style. Fine.

`src/app/core/messages.service.ts`:

```ts
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { Subject } from 'rxjs';  // rxjs available ✓

export type MessageStyle = 'SUCCESS' | 'ERROR';

export interface AppMessage {
  text: string;
  style: MessageStyle;
}

@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class MessagesService {
  readonly messages = <signal approach?> 
}
```

Angular 18: signals available. Use a signal-based store:

```ts
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class MessagesService {
  private nextId = 0;
  private readonly _messages = signal<MessageEntry[]>([]);
  readonly messages = this._messages.asReadonly();

  success(text: string): void { this.add(text, 'SUCCESS'); }
  error(text: string): void { this.add(text, 'ERROR'); }

  private add(text: string, style: MessageStyle): void {
    const entry: MessageEntry = { entryId: ++this.nextId, text, style };
    this._messages.update((list) => [...list, entry]);
    setTimeout(() => this.close(entry), 5000);
  }

  close(entry: MessageEntry): void {
    this._messages.update((list) => list.filter((m) => m !== entry));
  }
}
```

5000ms default like GWT MessageCloseDelay.DEFAULT.

Hmm — signal + setTimeout in zone: after timeout, CD ✓. If zoneless... we have zone.js ✓.

Message id: entryId used for tracking (@for track). DOM id is per style: 'gwt-debug-successMessage' / 'gwt-debug-errorMessage' — duplicates allowed (GWT did that with ensureDebugId on each widget).

**messages.component.ts** (widget/message + MessagesView):

```html
<div class="messages-wrapper">
  @for (message of messages(); track message.entryId) {
    <div class="message" [class.success]="..." [class.error]="..." [id]="message.style === 'SUCCESS' ? 'gwt-debug-successMessage' : 'gwt-debug-errorMessage'">
      <span>{{ message.text }}</span>
      <span class="close" (click)="close(message)">X</span>
    </div>
  }
</div>
```

Wait — `messageContains` does `container.findElement(By.cssSelector("span"))` → first span = text ✓. `hideMessage`: `message.findElement(By.cssSelector("span:last-child"))` → the close span must be the LAST CHILD of the container (span:last-child matches a span that is the last child). My structure: text span + close span as the only two children ✓. Click → close(message) → removed from array → *removed from DOM* → stalenessOf ✓ (used by hideMessage → waitUntilElementIsDetached).

Note: `[id]` binding with fixed strings — use plain `id="gwt-debug-successMessage"` in a branch? Two separate divs (one for success, one for error) is cleaner:

```html
@if (m.style === 'SUCCESS') { <div id="gwt-debug-successMessage" ...> } @else { <div id="gwt-debug-errorMessage" ...> }
```

Hmm, but @if inside @for with the spans duplicated... fine. Or single div with [attr.id]. `[id]="..."` works as property/attr binding (id is a DOM property, Angular reflects it). I'll use [id].

Wait, one more consideration: the container div — GWT MessageWidget: HTMLPanel (div) with class `message` + success/error class; visible=false initially then fadeIn. My div: visible immediately ✓.

**header.component.ts**:

